  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-Combating Food Insecurity in Uganda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project reflects the urgent need to reduce food insecurity of the most vulnerable population. It supports a wide range of activities that promote climate-resilient food production and income of small-scale food producers as well as food availabilityin local markets; increase most vulnerable people&#x2019;s immediate access to food through cash-based programs; and enhance nutrition of particularly children in Uganda.

The project complies with the OECD/DAC criteria for Official Development Assistance.

Objectives and target group
Impact Objective: Improve food security through increased access and availability of nutritious food for 17,000 most vulnerable individuals in Uganda.

Specific Objective 1: Increased local climate-resilient food productionand income of small-scale food producers in targeted areas;
Specific Objective 2: Reduction in scale of the hunger crisis and malnutrition in targeted areas
The Theoryof Change is a hierarchy of interdependent pathways of change aligned with Norad&#x2019;s strategy for promoting food security and Oxfam and partners&#x2019; strategies and country priorities:

IF small-scale food producers (and farmer groups) increase capacity to adopt climate resilient techniques and IF they are equipped with agricultural tools and inputs and IF local infrastructure is rehabilitated to support climate-resilient food production, and IF women and youth are supported to engage in income-generating activities THEN the local climate-resilient food production and income of food producers will increase and gender inequalities reduced. A key assumption of the pathway to change is that the agricultural cycle is not critically interrupted by e.g. conflicts and extreme weather.

IF vulnerable groups, in particular women and refugee-led households, receive cash transfers and nutritional inputs, and IF key education and health institutions and their staff increase awareness of prevention and management of nutrition and IF communities are engaged in local actions to mitigate climate change impact, THEN the scale of the hunger crisis and malnutrition will be reduced and negativecoping strategies minimized. Key assumptions of the pathway to change are that local markets are accessible and functional, and that local stakeholders are willing tocommit to involvement and engagement with communities.</narrative>
  </description>
